"""Scene definitions for the Input Validation with Hibernate Validator teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Input Validation with Hibernate Validator',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains input validation with Hibernate Validator, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Input validation means deciding what is allowed, and refusing everything else, at the point where data comes in. [[slnc 300]] With Hibernate Validator, you write each rule once, as an annotation on a type, and the library runs it at the boundary, in an order you choose, with the cheap limits first. [[slnc 300]] But a built-in rule can be wider than yours, and valid text is still not safe text. [[slnc 600]] Think of a checkout clerk with a printed list of what each box on the form may hold. [[slnc 600]] Our example is an online store.'),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online store takes a checkout form:', '  an e-mail address', '  a country and a postal code', '  the lines of a cart', '', 'The demo writes the rules once,', 'as annotations on Java records,', 'and sends good and bad forms through them.'],
         narration='Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online store takes a checkout form: an e-mail address, a country and a postal code, and the lines of a cart, each with a product code and a quantity. [[slnc 500]] The demo writes the rules once, as annotations on Java records. [[slnc 300]] Then it sends good forms and bad forms through them, and counts what each one costs.'),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Constraint: a rule written as an annotation', 'Violation: a broken rule, with a path and a message', 'Group sequence: the order the checks run in', 'Message template: the text of a violation', 'Expression language: code inside a template'],
         narration="Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] A constraint is a rule, written as an annotation on a field or a parameter. [[slnc 500]] A violation is one broken rule, with the path of the value and a message. [[slnc 500]] A group sequence is the order in which groups of checks run. [[slnc 500]] A message template is the text of a violation, with places for values. [[slnc 500]] And the expression language is code that a template can run, which is why a visitor's text must never become a template."),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['SKU: Stock Keeping Unit: a product code, such as SHO-1042', 'JDK: Java Development Kit', 'ISO: International Organization for Standardization', 'NFC: Unicode Normalization Form C', "EL: Expression Language: Jakarta's language for code in templates", 'ReDoS: Regular expression Denial of Service'],
         narration="A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] S K U is stock keeping unit: the store's code for one product. [[slnc 400]] J D K is the Java development kit: the compiler, the libraries and the regular expression engine. [[slnc 400]] I S O is the International Organization for Standardization, which lists the two-letter country codes. [[slnc 400]] N F C is Unicode normalization form C: one way to write each accented letter. [[slnc 400]] E L is the expression language: code inside a message template, such as dollar, brace, one plus one. [[slnc 400]] Re Dos is regular expression denial of service: a pattern that takes so long on a crafted input that it stops a server."),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['U+0061: A Unicode code point in hexadecimal: here the letter a'],
         narration='U plus, then four hexadecimal digits, names one Unicode character, for example U plus zero zero six one is the letter a.'),
    dict(key='06-one-rule', kind='figure', title='One rule, written on the type',
         body='one_rule',
         narration="Let's see the core idea on one example: the quantity of a cart line. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the rule, written once as annotations on the record: Min one and Max ninety nine. [[slnc 300]] The demo reads it back from the code. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the input: three quantities, seven, zero and one hundred. [[slnc 300]] On the right, the result. [[slnc 300]] Seven is accepted. [[slnc 300]] Zero breaks the Min rule, and one hundred breaks the Max rule, and each violation carries the constraint's own message. [[slnc 700]] The same annotations run wherever the record is checked: at the controller, at the service, or in a test."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='The constraints travel with the type to the service',
         body='boundary',
         narration="First demo: where validation belongs. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the input: a checkout request with a quantity of minus three, sent without the page. [[slnc 300]] There is no price field: the catalogue has the price. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, before the fix: a shop that trusts the page. [[slnc 300]] Eighty nine ninety times minus three is minus two hundred and sixty nine seventy. [[slnc 300]] A refund. [[slnc 700]] On the right, after the fix. [[slnc 300]] The service's parameter carries the constraints, so the validator refuses the request. [[slnc 300]] The violation names its path, part by part: the method place, its parameter checkout, the list lines, element zero, and the field quantity."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — Where validation belongs',
         body='ONE. Where validation belongs.\n  THE REQUEST, SENT WITHOUT THE PAGE:\n    cart         SHO-1042 x -3\n    fields       email, country, postalCode, login, lines\n                 (no price field: the catalogue has the price)\n  BEFORE THE FIX (a shop that trusts the page):\n    total        89.90 x -3 = -269.70  <- A REFUND',
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The request: one line, S H O ten forty two, quantity minus three. [[slnc 300]] The form's fields have no price. [[slnc 400]] Before the fix, the trusting shop totals it as a refund."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — Where validation belongs',
         body="ONE. (continued)\n  AFTER THE FIX (constraints on the service's parameter):\n    cart -3      place.checkout.lines[0].quantity\n                 must be greater than or equal to 1\n    no form      place.checkout: must not be null\n    honest       accepted, 89.90 x 2 = 179.80\n  HOW THE PATH place.checkout.lines[0].quantity IS BUILT:\n    place        method\n    checkout     parameter\n    lines        property\n    quantity     property, element 0 of the list before it\n    message      from @Min(1) on Line.quantity",
         narration='After the fix, the service refuses it, with a path and a message. [[slnc 300]] A missing form is refused too, and an honest order is totalled from the catalogue. [[slnc 400]] How the path is built: the method, its parameter, the list, and the field of element zero. [[slnc 300]] The message comes from the Min annotation on the quantity.'),
    dict(key='10-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='The rule on the type, the input, the result',
         body='rules_results',
         narration="Second demo: say what is allowed, in annotations. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the rules, read back from the records themselves. [[slnc 300]] A quantity has Min one and Max ninety nine. [[slnc 300]] A product code has a pattern: three capitals, a dash, four digits. [[slnc 300]] An e-mail address has a size limit and the built-in Email check. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the input values, and on the right, the result. [[slnc 300]] Green is accepted, rose is refused, with the constraint's own message. [[slnc 500]] And amber is a warning. [[slnc 300]] The built-in Email check accepts ben at localhost, an address the shop cannot deliver to. [[slnc 300]] A built-in rule can be wider than yours."),
    dict(key='11-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — Say what is allowed, in annotations',
         body='TWO. Say what is allowed, in annotations.\n  THE RULES, WRITTEN ON THE RECORDS:\n    quantity     @Min(1) @Max(99)\n    sku          @NotNull @Pattern([A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}) @InStock\n    email        @NotBlank @Size(max=254) @Email\n    country      @NotNull @Country\n  QUANTITY (THE INPUT, THEN THE RESULT):\n    7                    accepted\n    0                    refused: must be greater than or equal to 1\n    100                  refused: must be less than or equal to 99',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] First the rules, read from the annotations on the records. [[slnc 300]] Then quantities: seven passes, zero and one hundred are refused, each with its message.'),
    dict(key='12-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — Say what is allowed, in annotations',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  SKU:\n    "SHO-1042"           accepted\n    "sho-1042"           refused: must match "[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}"\n    "SHO-1042;"          refused: must match "[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}"\n  EMAIL:\n    "Ben@Shop.EXAMPLE"   accepted\n    "ben@@shop.example"  refused: must be a well-formed email address\n    "ben@localhost"      accepted  <- WIDER THAN THE SHOP NEEDS\n  COUNTRY:\n    "DE"   accepted\n    "ZZ"   refused: a two-letter country code such as IN or DE\n    "de"   refused: a two-letter country code such as IN or DE',
         narration='Product codes must match the pattern. [[slnc 300]] An e-mail address with two at signs is refused, but ben at localhost is accepted by the built-in check. [[slnc 300]] Country codes must be real and in capitals.'),
    dict(key='13-act-2-3', kind='console', title='Act Two — Say what is allowed, in annotations',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  THE INPUT, A FORM WITH SEVERAL BAD FIELDS:\n    email        ben@@shop.example\n    country      ZZ\n    login        Ben K\n    cart         sho-1042 x 0\n  THE RESULT, 5 PROBLEMS AT ONCE:\n    country: a two-letter country code such as IN or DE\n    email: must be a well-formed email address\n    lines[0].quantity: must be greater than or equal to 1\n    lines[0].sku: must match "[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}"\n    login: must match "[a-z0-9._-]+"',
         narration='Last, a form with several bad fields. [[slnc 300]] The validator reports all five violations at once, each with the path of its field. [[slnc 300]] The customer fixes everything in one go.'),
    dict(key='14-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='Cheap checks first: a lookup only for a good shape',
         body='group_order',
         narration='Third demo: your own constraints, and an order. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the group sequence. [[slnc 300]] First the Limits group: sizes, before anything looks inside a value. [[slnc 300]] Then the Default group: the shapes, such as patterns. [[slnc 300]] Last the Expensive group: the check that asks the catalogue. [[slnc 300]] A group runs only if every group before it found nothing wrong. [[slnc 700]] Below, the input: two product codes. [[slnc 300]] Lower-case s h o ten forty two has the wrong shape. [[slnc 300]] It stops in the Default group, and the catalogue is asked zero times. [[slnc 500]] S H O ninety nine ninety nine has the right shape, so it reaches the lookup. [[slnc 300]] One lookup, and it is not in the catalogue.'),
    dict(key='15-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — Your own constraints, across fields, and in an order',
         body="THREE. Your own constraints, across fields, and in an order.\n  THE INPUT, FIVE LOGINS:\n    ben[U+200B]k      a zero-width space\n    ben[U+000A]k      a line break\n    BenK              capitals\n    b[U+0435]n.k      Cyrillic е in place of e\n    jose[U+0301]      é typed as two characters\n  THE RESULT:          @NoInvisible  all the login's rules\n    ben[U+200B]k      refused       refused\n    ben[U+000A]k      refused       refused\n    BenK              accepted      refused\n    b[U+0435]n.k      accepted      refused\n    jose[U+0301]      refused       refused\n    caught       3 of 5 by @NoInvisible, 5 of 5 by all the rules",
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] Five logins, with their hidden characters made visible. [[slnc 300]] The invisible character rule alone catches three of them. [[slnc 300]] Capitals and a Cyrillic letter pass it, and are caught by the pattern. [[slnc 300]] Together, the rules catch all five.'),
    dict(key='16-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — Your own constraints, across fields, and in an order',
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  THE INPUT, A RULE ACROSS TWO FIELDS:\n    DE 1011      DE codes match \\d{5}\n    IN 110001    IN codes match \\d{6}\n  THE RESULT, ON THE POSTAL CODE:\n    DE 1011      postalCode: not a postal code of that country\n    IN 110001    accepted',
         narration='A rule across two fields. [[slnc 300]] A German postal code has five digits, so ten eleven is refused. [[slnc 300]] The violation is reported on the postal code, where the person can fix it. [[slnc 300]] The Indian code with six digits is accepted.'),
    dict(key='17-act-3-3', kind='console', title='Act Three — Your own constraints, across fields, and in an order',
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  HOW THE GROUPS ORDER THE CHECKS (each runs if all before passed):\n    1 Limits     sizes, before anything looks inside\n    2 Default    shapes: @Pattern, @Email, @Min ...\n    3 Expensive  @InStock: a catalogue lookup\n  THE INPUT, TWO PRODUCT CODES:\n    sho-1042     the wrong shape\n    SHO-9999     the right shape, but not sold\n  THE RESULT:\n    sho-1042     0 lookups: must match "[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}"\n    SHO-9999     1 lookup:  not in the catalogue',
         narration='The group sequence: limits, then shapes, then lookups. [[slnc 300]] The wrong shape costs no lookup. [[slnc 300]] The right shape that nobody sells costs one.'),
    dict(key='18-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='A size limit first, so the slow pattern never runs',
         body='redos_growth',
         narration="Fourth demo: limits are validation too. [[slnc 400]] The input: a run of a's, then an exclamation mark, checked against a pattern with repetitions nested two deep. [[slnc 700]] The rose bars are before the fix. [[slnc 300]] Eight a's take about two thousand steps. [[slnc 300]] Twelve take thirty six thousand. [[slnc 300]] Sixteen take almost six hundred thousand. [[slnc 300]] Four more a's, sixteen times the work. [[slnc 700]] The green bar is after the fix. [[slnc 300]] A size limit of twenty, in the first group, refuses five thousand a's before the pattern runs: zero steps. [[slnc 500]] The amber bars show the simpler pattern on the J D K. [[slnc 300]] It grows slowly, which is why a safe looking pattern is not proof of a safe pattern."),
    dict(key='19-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — Limits are validation too',
         body="FOUR. Limits are validation too.\n  THE INPUT, TWO OVERSIZED VALUES:\n    cart         10000 lines\n    e-mail       10013 characters\n  THE RESULT, STOPPED IN THE Limits GROUP:\n    cart         lines: size must be between 0 and 50, 0 lookups\n    e-mail       email: size must be between 0 and 254\n  BEFORE THE FIX, ((a+)+)+b ON n a's AND A !:\n    8 a's        2264 steps\n    12 a's       36808 steps  (x 16.3)\n    16 a's       589752 steps  (x 16.0)\n    4 more a's: about 2^4 = 16 times the work  <- ReDoS",
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] A cart of ten thousand lines and a very long e-mail address both stop in the limits group, before any lookup. [[slnc 400]] Then the nested pattern. [[slnc 300]] Four more a's multiply the steps by about sixteen. [[slnc 300]] That is regular expression denial of service."),
    dict(key='20-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — Limits are validation too',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  AFTER THE FIX, @Size(max = 20) IN THE Limits GROUP:\n    5000 a's     code: size must be between 0 and 20, 0 steps\n  WHY TWO LEVELS: THE JDK ON THE SIMPLER (a+)+$:\n    8 a's        109 steps\n    16 a's       349 steps  (x 3.2)\n    32 a's       1213 steps  (x 3.5)\n    twice the a's, 3 to 4 times the work: not doubling per a",
         narration="After the fix, a size limit in the first group refuses five thousand a's in zero steps. [[slnc 400]] And the simpler pattern: twice the a's cost three to four times the steps, not doubling for each a. [[slnc 300]] One more level of nesting is enough to make it exponential."),
    dict(key='21-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title="A visitor's text inside a message template",
         body='template_injection',
         narration="Fifth demo: the bill. [[slnc 400]] Validation does not do everything. [[slnc 600]] At the top, the input: a coupon code typed by a visitor, dollar, brace, one plus one, brace. [[slnc 300]] A careless constraint puts it inside its message template. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, before the fix: a validator that lets templates call methods, as old versions did. [[slnc 300]] The template is evaluated, and the message says the coupon two does not exist. [[slnc 300]] The visitor's text was run as code. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, after the fix. [[slnc 300]] Hibernate Validator nine does not evaluate it by default. [[slnc 300]] And passed as a message parameter, the text stays text, even on the risky validator."),
    dict(key='22-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill: what validation does not do',
         body="FIVE. The bill: what validation does not do.\n  THE INPUT, A REVIEW TITLE:\n    title        <b>great</b> (12 characters)\n  THE RESULT, A MESSAGE THAT REPEATS THE VALUE:\n    title: '<b>great</b>' is not 3 to 10 characters\n    repeats it?  true  <- MARKUP IN AN ERROR MESSAGE\n  THE INPUT, A COUPON PUT INTO A MESSAGE TEMPLATE:\n    ${1+1}\n    ${'a'.concat('b')}",
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] A review title with a bold tag is too long. [[slnc 300]] The message repeats the title, markup and all. [[slnc 300]] If an error page prints it, the markup runs. [[slnc 400]] Then two coupons that look like template expressions.'),
    dict(key='23-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill: what validation does not do',
         body="FIVE. (continued)\n  BEFORE THE FIX (templates may call methods, as old versions did):\n    ${1+1}              the coupon 2 does not exist\n    ${'a'.concat('b')}  the coupon ab does not exist\n    the visitor's text was run as code  <- EXPRESSION INJECTION\n  AFTER THE FIX:\n    default      the coupon ${1+1} does not exist\n    parameter    the coupon ${1+1} does not exist\n  SO:\n    valid text still needs encoding on output and query parameters;\n    each rule is a business decision, written down",
         narration='Before the fix, the templates are evaluated: one plus one becomes two, and a method call joins a and b. [[slnc 300]] After the fix, the default and a message parameter keep the text as it was typed. [[slnc 400]] Valid text still needs encoding and query parameters, and every rule is a business decision.'),
    dict(key='24-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['The rules live in annotations, far from the page', 'Built-in constraints are general', 'A custom constraint is code to test', 'Messages are output, and can carry markup', 'The order of checks must be written down'],
         narration="Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] The rules live in annotations, away from the page, so both must be kept in step. [[slnc 500]] Built-in constraints are general, and may allow more than your rule does. [[slnc 500]] A custom constraint is code, and needs tests of its own. [[slnc 500]] Error messages are output, and can carry the visitor's markup. [[slnc 300]] And the order of the checks must be written down, as a group sequence."),
    dict(key='25-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['@Valid and @NotNull on a parameter', '@Size, @Pattern or @Email on a field', 'A custom @Constraint annotation', 'A 400 response that lists field errors'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] Valid and not null annotations on a method parameter are validation at the service boundary. [[slnc 500]] Size, pattern, or e-mail annotations on a field are the rules written on the type. [[slnc 500]] A custom constraint annotation is a rule of your own, like the postal code check. [[slnc 500]] And a four hundred response that lists every field error is all the violations, reported at once.'),
    dict(key='26-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Constraints on every type that crosses the boundary', 'Validate at the service, not only the page', 'Sizes, then shapes, then lookups', 'Your own pattern over a wide built-in', 'No raw input in messages or templates', 'Valid is not the same as safe'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Put constraints on every type that crosses the boundary. [[slnc 300]] Validate at the service, not only on the page. [[slnc 500]] Check sizes first, then shapes, then anything that costs a lookup. [[slnc 500]] Write a pattern for exactly what you require, instead of trusting a wide built-in. [[slnc 300]] Keep raw input out of messages and templates. [[slnc 500]] And remember that valid text is not safe text: encode it for wherever it goes next.'),
    dict(key='27-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['Checkout: the form, as a record, with its constraints', 'Line: one line of the cart', 'Orders: the service, validated at its boundary', 'Basic: the validation groups and their order', 'NoInvisible: a custom constraint: no hidden characters', 'PostalCodeMatchesCountry: a rule across two fields', 'Coupon: a slow pattern behind a size limit', "UnsafeMessage: the visitor's text in a template, and the safe way"],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] The rules are annotations on records, and a few small classes add our own rules and the order. [[slnc 300]] Here are the ones that matter.'),
    dict(key='28-class-1', kind='code', title='Checkout — the rules on the type',
         body='// Checkout: the form, as a record, with its constraints\n@NotBlank\n@Size(max = 254, groups = Basic.Limits.class)\n@Email String email,\n...\n@NotEmpty\n@Size(max = 50, groups = Basic.Limits.class)\nList<@NotNull @Valid Line> lines) {',
         narration='Each field carries its rules. [[slnc 300]] Size limits are in the Limits group, so they run first. [[slnc 300]] Valid on the list makes the validator check every line of the cart.'),
    dict(key='29-class-2', kind='code', title='Line — a shape, a lookup, a range',
         body='// Line: one line of the cart\n@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "[A-Z]{3}-\\\\d{4}")\n@InStock(groups = Basic.Expensive.class)\nString sku,\n@Min(1) @Max(99) int quantity) {',
         narration='The product code has a pattern, and the catalogue lookup is in the Expensive group, so it runs last. [[slnc 300]] The quantity is from one to ninety nine.'),
    dict(key='30-class-3', kind='code', title='Orders — constraints on the parameter',
         body='// Orders: the service, validated at its boundary\npublic BigDecimal place(@NotNull @Valid Checkout checkout) {',
         narration="The service's own parameter says: not null, and valid. [[slnc 300]] So nothing past this line ever sees an unchecked form, whoever calls it."),
    dict(key='31-class-4', kind='code', title='Basic — limits, then shapes, then lookups',
         body='// Basic: the validation groups and their order\n@GroupSequence({Limits.class, Default.class, Expensive.class})\npublic interface Ordered {',
         narration='A group sequence runs the groups in order. [[slnc 300]] A group runs only if all the groups before it passed, so a bad shape never costs a lookup.'),
    dict(key='32-class-5', kind='code', title='NoInvisible — normal form C, and no control or format characters',
         body='// NoInvisible: a custom constraint: no hidden characters\nif (!Normalizer.isNormalized(value, Normalizer.Form.NFC)) {\n    return false;\n...\nreturn value.codePoints().map(Character::getType)\n        .noneMatch(type -> type == Character.CONTROL || type == Character.FORMAT',
         narration="Text that is not in normal form C is refused, so what is checked is what is stored. [[slnc 300]] Then every character's type is checked: no control or format characters, such as a zero width space."),
    dict(key='33-class-6', kind='code', title='PostalCodeMatchesCountry — report it on the postal code',
         body='// PostalCodeMatchesCountry: a rule across two fields\nString template = context.getDefaultConstraintMessageTemplate();\ncontext.buildConstraintViolationWithTemplate(template)\n        .addPropertyNode("postalCode")\n        .addConstraintViolation();',
         narration='The rule sits on the whole record, because it needs two fields. [[slnc 300]] But the violation is put on the postal code, where the person can fix it.'),
    dict(key='34-class-7', kind='code', title='Coupon — the size first, the pattern last',
         body='// Coupon: a slow pattern behind a size limit\n@Size(max = 20, groups = Basic.Limits.class)\n@Redos(groups = Basic.Expensive.class)',
         narration='The size limit is in the first group, the slow pattern in the last. [[slnc 300]] A long coupon is refused before the pattern ever reads it.'),
    dict(key='35-class-8', kind='code', title='UnsafeMessage — inside the template, or as a parameter',
         body='// UnsafeMessage: the visitor\'s text in a template, and the safe way\nString template = "the coupon " + value + " does not exist";\ncontext.buildConstraintViolationWithTemplate(template).addConstraintViolation();\n...\n        .addMessageParameter("coupon", value)\n        .buildConstraintViolationWithTemplate("the coupon {coupon} does not exist")',
         narration="The unsafe version glues the visitor's text into the template, which is then evaluated. [[slnc 300]] The safe version passes it as a message parameter, which stays text."),
    dict(key='36-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a @Pattern to the e-mail', 'so that it refuses ben@localhost.'],
         narration="That's input validation with Hibernate Validator. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] A rule that is written once, on the type, and checked in order from the cheapest to the dearest, is a rule that gets used. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a pattern to the e-mail field, so that it refuses an address with no dot in its domain. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

VIDEO = {'footer': 'Input Validation with Hibernate Validator  ·  Security Basics  ·  Java', 'poster': {'headline': [('VALIDATION WITH', 'text'), ('HIBERNATE VALIDATOR', 'accent')], 'taglines': [('Write what is allowed once, as annotations on the type, and let Jakarta Validation run it ', 'gold')], 'before': {'code': 'trusting the page: quantity -3 -> total -269.70', 'note': 'Where validation belongs', 'tag': 'BEFORE'}, 'after': {'code': '@Size(max = 20, groups = Limits.class)', 'note': 'The bill: what validation does not do', 'tag': 'AFTER'}}, 'highlight': {'console_green': ['AFTER THE FIX', 'THE RESULT'], 'console_rose': ['BEFORE THE FIX'], 'console_amber': ['HOW ', 'WHY '], 'console_cyan': ['THE INPUT', 'THE REQUEST', 'THE RULES', 'QUANTITY', 'SKU', 'EMAIL', 'COUNTRY', 'SO:']}}
